1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,430
You open a terminal. Someone walks past.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,280
They see a black screen
covered in mysterious text.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,930
And suddenly...

4
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:10,105
"Oh my god! You're hacking!"

5
00:00:12,045 --> 00:00:16,795
Well... Maybe. Or maybe you are just
trying to find out why the server is down.

6
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:19,763
Today we are going to look
at why terminals and hacking

7
00:00:19,843 --> 00:00:22,843
have become almost the same
thing in popular culture.

8
00:00:22,923 --> 00:00:24,853
And, more importantly...

9
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:27,513
what hacking actually looks like.

10
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:34,783
For most people, a computer has buttons.
You click something. Something happens.

11
00:00:35,673 --> 00:00:40,443
Then there is the terminal. No buttons.
No friendly messages. Just this.

12
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:42,233
And that is terrifying.

13
00:00:43,135 --> 00:00:46,005
A graphical interface
might say: delete user.

14
00:00:46,755 --> 00:00:48,505
A terminal says this instead.

15
00:00:49,377 --> 00:00:52,787
To an experienced Linux user,
that is perfectly ordinary.

16
00:00:53,837 --> 00:00:56,847
To everyone else, it looks like
you are about to launch a missile.

17
00:00:59,527 --> 00:01:02,777
There is actually a historical
reason for the way it looks.

18
00:01:03,467 --> 00:01:06,077
Early computers had no
graphical interface.

19
00:01:06,807 --> 00:01:08,997
People talked to them
through text terminals.

20
00:01:09,889 --> 00:01:14,252
And those terminals had dark screens
with green or amber phosphor text.

21
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:16,799
That was not an attempt to look cool.

22
00:01:17,429 --> 00:01:19,599
It was simply how the technology worked.

23
00:01:20,399 --> 00:01:23,269
But then film and television
noticed something useful.

24
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:27,669
A black screen full of rapidly
changing text looks technical.

25
00:01:27,899 --> 00:01:31,529
And, more importantly...
it looks complicated.

26
00:01:32,647 --> 00:01:36,677
You do not need to understand what the
characters mean. You just see this.

27
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:40,797
And your brain decides: this
person is doing something illegal.

28
00:01:43,697 --> 00:01:47,367
Hollywood has developed a remarkably
consistent language for hacking.

29
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:52,007
The hacker sits in a dark
room. There are six monitors.

30
00:01:53,013 --> 00:01:55,383
The room is lit entirely by the monitors.

31
00:01:56,333 --> 00:01:58,223
Nobody has turned the lights on.

32
00:01:58,993 --> 00:02:02,283
The hacker starts typing at roughly
nine hundred words a minute.

33
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:04,743
Text flies across the screen.

34
00:02:05,253 --> 00:02:06,313
A progress bar appears.

35
00:02:06,393 --> 00:02:09,910
Ninety-seven per cent.
Ninety-eight. Ninety-nine.

36
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:14,323
And finally, one hundred. The
hacker leans back. And says...

37
00:02:15,124 --> 00:02:16,324
"I'm in."

38
00:02:17,876 --> 00:02:21,186
That is not how most computer
security incidents work.

39
00:02:22,016 --> 00:02:23,886
Let us imagine the real conversation.

40
00:02:25,036 --> 00:02:26,666
"I am going to hack this server."

41
00:02:27,416 --> 00:02:29,786
And the server answers: permission denied.

42
00:02:30,735 --> 00:02:33,985
"Oh." Tries again. Permission
denied. Checks the password.

43
00:02:34,863 --> 00:02:36,373
Reads the documentation.

44
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:38,893
Searches the error message.

45
00:02:39,003 --> 00:02:41,423
Eventually discovers
that the administrator

46
00:02:41,503 --> 00:02:44,613
turned off password logins six months ago.

47
00:02:45,374 --> 00:02:46,574
"Right."

48
00:02:49,062 --> 00:02:52,122
Computers are remarkably good at
making you spend twenty minutes

49
00:02:52,202 --> 00:02:55,972
discovering that you forgot one
character in a configuration file.

50
00:02:58,802 --> 00:03:03,772
On one side of the screen: system
administrator. On the other: hacker.

51
00:03:04,582 --> 00:03:06,632
The same tools can appear on both.

52
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:11,332
That is because the terminal is not
a hacking tool. It is an interface.

53
00:03:12,303 --> 00:03:16,123
You can use it to run a server,
read logs, copy files, install

54
00:03:16,203 --> 00:03:19,653
software, or work out what
broke at three in the morning.

55
00:03:20,357 --> 00:03:22,417
Or, depending on what you
are doing and whether you

56
00:03:22,497 --> 00:03:27,707
have permission, you can use it for
security testing. Or for an attack.

57
00:03:28,561 --> 00:03:30,991
The tool does not tell you
which one you are doing.

58
00:03:31,821 --> 00:03:33,831
A screwdriver can repair a computer.

59
00:03:34,741 --> 00:03:36,451
It can also destroy one.

60
00:03:37,081 --> 00:03:38,871
The screwdriver is not the criminal.

61
00:03:41,791 --> 00:03:43,461
So what actually is a hacker?

62
00:03:44,311 --> 00:03:47,521
The word has a much older
history than modern cybercrime.

63
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:50,451
Originally, a hacker
was someone who liked to

64
00:03:50,531 --> 00:03:54,041
explore systems and could
find unexpected solutions.

65
00:03:54,999 --> 00:03:57,939
Over time, the word became
attached to computer security.

66
00:03:58,019 --> 00:04:02,709
And today, when someone says "he
is a hacker", they might mean

67
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:07,531
a security researcher, a penetration
tester, a genuine attacker,

68
00:04:07,721 --> 00:04:10,811
or simply someone who knows an
alarming amount about Linux.

69
00:04:11,661 --> 00:04:13,671
Usually the context helps.

70
00:04:16,271 --> 00:04:18,381
Now let us look at our picture again.

71
00:04:19,071 --> 00:04:22,381
The caption says: you hacked
it. And look at her screens.

72
00:04:23,304 --> 00:04:25,334
On one of them, something like nmap.

73
00:04:25,664 --> 00:04:28,754
On the other, something that
looks even more suspicious.

74
00:04:29,324 --> 00:04:31,174
Slash etc slash passwd.

75
00:04:32,513 --> 00:04:36,783
To someone who does not know Linux, she
is clearly breaking into the Pentagon.

76
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:39,833
To a system administrator, she is checking

77
00:04:39,913 --> 00:04:42,143
the network and reading
a list of accounts.

78
00:04:43,129 --> 00:04:45,199
And that is where the joke comes from.

79
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:48,099
The woman may not be
hacking anything at all.

80
00:04:48,769 --> 00:04:50,559
She might just be doing her job.

81
00:04:51,271 --> 00:04:53,441
So what is nmap actually doing?

82
00:04:54,271 --> 00:04:57,581
It is a tool for network
discovery and security auditing.

83
00:04:58,324 --> 00:05:00,824
In very simple terms, you
point it at a machine and

84
00:05:00,904 --> 00:05:04,374
ask: what is this computer
exposing to the network?

85
00:05:05,332 --> 00:05:07,352
It might tell you that
certain ports are open.

86
00:05:07,432 --> 00:05:10,922
And ports correspond to network services.

87
00:05:11,872 --> 00:05:13,842
That is not automatically an attack.

88
00:05:14,648 --> 00:05:18,118
It is more like looking at a building
and asking which doors are open.

89
00:05:18,988 --> 00:05:21,878
If it is your building, that
is normal administration.

90
00:05:23,023 --> 00:05:26,873
If it is someone else's, and you are
deliberately looking for a way in...

91
00:05:27,557 --> 00:05:29,207
well, that is a different story.

92
00:05:31,897 --> 00:05:33,287
And what about that path?

93
00:05:33,997 --> 00:05:36,787
Someone might think: she is
reading the password file.

94
00:05:37,617 --> 00:05:39,787
It does contain
information about accounts.

95
00:05:40,807 --> 00:05:45,417
But on a modern Linux system, the actual
password hashes are stored somewhere else.

96
00:05:46,347 --> 00:05:50,437
They are in slash etc slash shadow,
which is much more restricted.

97
00:05:51,401 --> 00:05:55,951
So seeing that file is not the same thing
as discovering everyone's passwords.

98
00:05:56,701 --> 00:05:59,411
Hollywood, of course, would
make this much more dramatic.

99
00:06:00,438 --> 00:06:04,528
A Linux administrator would say:
it is literally a list of users.

100
00:06:05,441 --> 00:06:09,291
Another classic scene cracks a password
in approximately fourteen seconds.

101
00:06:10,281 --> 00:06:11,951
Real cracking is duller.

102
00:06:12,541 --> 00:06:16,551
You have a hash, you have a wordlist,
and you let the computer try candidates.

103
00:06:17,551 --> 00:06:19,341
Maybe it finds one in a minute.

104
00:06:19,431 --> 00:06:22,381
Maybe in a week. Maybe never.

105
00:06:22,511 --> 00:06:25,661
And while it works, the attacker
is not sitting there typing.

106
00:06:26,571 --> 00:06:28,341
They might be eating a sandwich.

107
00:06:29,133 --> 00:06:31,803
Which brings me to my
favourite Hollywood tradition.

108
00:06:32,893 --> 00:06:34,873
Apparently computers
do not accept commands

109
00:06:34,953 --> 00:06:37,423
unless you type them extremely fast.

110
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:43,850
Real programmers and administrators do not
type constantly. They think. They read.

111
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:45,690
They stare at an error.

112
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:49,690
Then they discover that someone
solved it eleven years ago.

113
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,210
And then they copy the solution.

114
00:06:53,066 --> 00:06:54,576
That is computer science.

115
00:06:57,396 --> 00:07:02,146
Sometimes the most dangerous thing you can
type is not a mysterious hacking command.

116
00:07:03,116 --> 00:07:05,066
It is something perfectly legitimate.

117
00:07:06,027 --> 00:07:08,177
The famous example is this one.

118
00:07:08,939 --> 00:07:12,789
Which is a wonderful story, except
that a modern system refuses.

119
00:07:13,999 --> 00:07:16,699
It answers that operating
recursively on slash is

120
00:07:16,779 --> 00:07:20,229
dangerous, unless you add
a flag saying you meant it.

121
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:23,730
The commands that really
destroy things are duller.

122
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:26,010
A delete in the wrong directory.

123
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,930
A disk written over the wrong disk.

124
00:07:29,767 --> 00:07:31,587
The terminal does not
warn you about those,

125
00:07:31,667 --> 00:07:34,317
because as far as it can
tell, nothing is wrong.

126
00:07:35,247 --> 00:07:38,017
It can only ask: are you absolutely sure?

127
00:07:38,782 --> 00:07:41,392
And if you answer yes...
it may believe you.

128
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:46,930
And here is the biggest problem
with the Hollywood stereotype.

129
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,830
A real attack might not
look like hacking at all.

130
00:07:51,534 --> 00:07:54,564
It might involve a stolen
password. Phishing.

131
00:07:55,074 --> 00:07:56,724
An unpatched server.

132
00:07:57,414 --> 00:07:58,864
A leaked API key.

133
00:07:59,414 --> 00:08:01,504
A password reused from somewhere else.

134
00:08:02,284 --> 00:08:04,434
The attacker might not
even need a terminal.

135
00:08:04,984 --> 00:08:09,344
They might simply log in to a web
interface. Username. Password.

136
00:08:09,424 --> 00:08:11,174
And now they are inside.

137
00:08:12,261 --> 00:08:14,881
No green text. No Matrix-style graphics.

138
00:08:14,961 --> 00:08:17,351
No dramatic music.

139
00:08:18,183 --> 00:08:20,153
Which is arguably much scarier.

140
00:08:22,793 --> 00:08:26,803
People imagine a hacker as a hoodie,
a dark room and a glowing keyboard.

141
00:08:27,533 --> 00:08:30,023
None of that has anything
to do with hacking.

142
00:08:30,912 --> 00:08:34,052
The person next to you on the
train, with a completely ordinary

143
00:08:34,132 --> 00:08:38,362
laptop, might know considerably
more about computer security.

144
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:41,940
And the person sitting in front
of six servers might simply

145
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:45,090
be trying to work out why
the database will not start.

146
00:08:47,850 --> 00:08:50,300
So let us return to our original picture.

147
00:08:51,170 --> 00:08:54,700
The man points at the woman.
You hacked it. Was she hacking?

148
00:08:55,588 --> 00:08:56,918
Maybe. Maybe not.

149
00:08:58,708 --> 00:09:01,018
She has a terminal. She has nmap.

150
00:09:01,328 --> 00:09:02,678
She has a list of accounts.

151
00:09:02,968 --> 00:09:06,458
But none of those things prove
that she has hacked anything.

152
00:09:07,168 --> 00:09:10,598
She could be the administrator,
or running a security audit.

153
00:09:11,354 --> 00:09:16,244
Or she could actually be hacking. We
do not know. And that is the joke.

154
00:09:18,950 --> 00:09:21,130
The real difference between
ordinary administration

155
00:09:21,210 --> 00:09:24,160
and hacking is not the
colour of your terminal.

156
00:09:24,870 --> 00:09:27,240
It is not how many monitors you have.

157
00:09:27,986 --> 00:09:29,796
It is not how fast you type.

158
00:09:30,166 --> 00:09:33,296
And it is certainly not whether
you are wearing a hoodie.

159
00:09:33,666 --> 00:09:37,056
It is what you are doing, and
whether you have permission to do it.

160
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:40,270
So next time you see someone
staring at a black screen

161
00:09:40,350 --> 00:09:44,120
full of mysterious text, do
not assume they are hacking.

162
00:09:44,908 --> 00:09:46,818
They might just be fixing the server.

163
00:09:47,713 --> 00:09:48,353
Although...

164
00:09:48,433 --> 00:09:52,063
if they are typing at nine hundred words
a minute while shouting "I'm in"...

165
00:09:52,737 --> 00:09:54,867
you might want to ask what they are doing.
